    "suppliedAnswer": "The course identifies four major family functions. Membership gives individual members a sense of belonging and a social identity and, at community level, contributes to the continuity of family structures. Economic support provides food, shelter, clothing and help during difficulties; this reduces some demands for external public assistance. Training transmits acceptable conduct, family values, practical skills and education, thereby preparing members to participate productively in society. Security protects members physically, psychologically and socially, helping to raise people capable of cooperating with their communities. These functions matter to family enterprises because the business draws on the family's human capacities, trust, shared norms and support. Quick Revision Obligation; culture; values; ethics; trust; procreation; membership; economic support; training; security. SUBTOPIC: Family Orientation, Values and Interdependency 3.1 Family Orientation, Values and Interdependency Family orientation describes the intensity of an individual's commitment to family relationships. The five dimensions in the course are tradition, stability, loyalty, trust and interdependency. Tradition preserves shared history, routines and legacy. Stability gives predictable behaviour and rules. Loyalty creates commitment and a willingness to put family interests into consideration. Trust makes cooperation less risky, while interdependency recognises the practical, emotional, financial and social support that relatives give one another. The dimensions are connected, not five unrelated slogans. A family's traditions and ethical rules encourage reliable conduct; reliability generates trust; trust encourages cohesion and exchange of support. These strengths can become business resources, particularly where close cooperation reduces the need for formal monitoring. However, a desire for stability may also make the family resist useful change, while loyalty can turn into pressure to retain unsuitable relatives in important positions. Traditions can include shared celebrations, family history, recognised responsibilities and routines. Stability comes from rules and boundaries that help members know what behaviour to expect. Loyalty promotes togetherness but can also create difficult obligations when personal and business interests differ. Trust means confidence that another family member will act fairly and keep commitments. Interdependency refers to the actual exchange of assistance. Taken together, the five dimensions help explain why business decisions among relatives may be influenced by previous family experiences as much as by immediate financial calculations. -- 4 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
