    "questionText": "Explain the Systems Theory and the Resource-Based View of family business.",
    "questionType": "written_structured",
    "marks": 20,
    "totalMarks": 20,
    "suppliedAnswer": "Systems theory treats the family enterprise as a collection of overlapping, interacting subsystems - family, ownership and management. A change in one subsystem affects others. It therefore helps explain conflicting responsibilities, succession issues and the need to coordinate family welfare with commercial goals. The Resource-Based View instead examines competitive advantage. It suggests that valuable, rare, hard-to-imitate and non-substitutable resources and capabilities can help one firm outperform others. In family businesses these resources may be trusted relationships, accumulated knowledge, reputation, enduring customer ties and entrepreneurial commitment. Systems theory explains interaction; RBV explains how distinctive capabilities may improve performance. Quick Revision Systems theory; subsystem interdependence; RBV; valuable, rare, inimitable and non-substitutable resources. -- 11 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
