    "questionText": "Discuss Social Capital Theory as applied to a family business.",
    "questionType": "written_structured",
    "marks": 20,
    "totalMarks": 20,
    "suppliedAnswer": "Social Capital Theory explains the productive benefits of relationships, common norms and networks. Within a family enterprise, relatives may share information quickly, offer finance, introduce customers, provide reliable labour and support collective goals without the same formality needed between strangers. Such relationships can make access to opportunities easier and lower some transaction costs. The theory is not a claim that every family link is good. Closed networks can restrict new knowledge or cause unfair appointments. The value of social capital comes from how it supports cooperation, good decisions and sustainable business activity. Quick Revision Trust; norms; networks; knowledge; collective action; connections. TOPIC 3: Agency Theory 3.1 Agency Theory Agency theory examines the relationship between principals, who own the enterprise, and agents, who manage it on their behalf. Problems arise when the two groups have different objectives, unequal information or incentives for self-serving conduct. Owners then spend resources on monitoring, reporting and control. Family ownership combined with family management may align interests and reduce some monitoring costs because the owner and manager are closely connected. If a family employs outsiders to manage the firm, oversight remains necessary; costs can rise when the family cannot monitor them effectively. The course also recognises that the absence of one sort of owner-manager conflict does not eliminate other conflicts among relatives. -- 12 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
