    "suppliedAnswer": "The material presents estimates indicating that family firms account for a very large proportion of businesses worldwide, with a range of about 80-90% in one cited data set and around two-thirds in another. It attributes major shares of employment, production and start-up finance to them. For the United States, it reports approximately 60% of GDP, substantial employment and job creation, and widespread family influence among both incorporated and publicly traded companies. These are estimates reported in the course, and the figures vary according to the definition of a family firm. They show why governance, continuity and succession planning affect the wider economy rather than owners alone. Quick Revision World estimates; USA statistics; employment; GDP; job creation; continuity. TOPIC 2: Notable Family Firms Across the World 3.1 Notable Family Firms Across the World Family businesses exist across banking, transport, retailing, manufacturing, technology and services. The material mentions prominent European, Asian and American examples, including Japanese enterprises known for long continuity and international groups such as Tata, Dabur, Reliance and the family-controlled businesses associated with global retail and industrial brands. Names of family enterprises are useful illustrations of the range of ownership structures, but the examination emphasis is usually on explaining what makes such a business family-influenced: retained strategic power, intergenerational involvement, family identity and governance. A huge company does not stop being a family firm merely because its employees and operations are spread across many countries. The history of family firms shows different routes to continuity. In some ventures, descendants remain close to daily management; in others, the family's role is principally exercised through ownership, boards and strategic oversight. The material includes businesses originating as local enterprises that grew into diversified groups. Their examples underline that family influence is compatible with major organisational scale. As a company grows, clearer structures and trained managers become essential because informal arrangements suitable for a small founder-led operation may no longer be adequate. PAST QUESTION - 2024_2 Q4",
