    "questionNumber": "POP Q18",
    "originalQuestionNumber": 18,
    "questionText": "Discuss the strengths and disadvantages of family businesses.",
    "questionType": "written_structured",
    "marks": 20,
    "totalMarks": 20,
    "suppliedAnswer": "Strengths include shared values, strong commitment, mutual trust, long-term thinking, stable relationships, tacit knowledge and a family reputation that can encourage cooperation with employees and customers. When ownership and management are aligned, some conflicts and monitoring costs may be lower. The disadvantages arise when family dynamics are mishandled: nepotism, sibling disagreements, inadequate succession, resistance to professional advice, difficulty retaining qualified outsiders, and confusion between the firm's assets and family demands. Good leadership aims to retain the useful family resources while introducing fair recruitment, clear accountability and plans for leadership transfer. Quick Revision Trust; reputation; loyalty; tacit knowledge; patient capital; resource-based advantage. TOPIC 2: Economic Policy, Liquidity and Finance 3.1 Economic Policy, Liquidity and Finance Growing family businesses must finance working capital, investment and at times the transfer of ownership across generations. The course identifies five ways of meeting liquidity needs: internally generated cash, additional funds from existing shareholders, broadening the shareholder group without listing, loans from insiders or external lenders, and selling non-core assets. The material also discusses the balance between equity and debt, reinvestment of profits and the possible financial pressure arising from inheritance or gift taxes and buy-outs of heirs who do not want involvement. Entrepreneurial training and ownership education help later generations understand both commercial operations and the responsibilities of controlling family capital. Cash generated from operations avoids immediate dilution of control but may be insufficient for a large investment. New shareholder contributions increase capital but place additional claims on future results. Outside borrowing provides funds while creating obligations to repay and pay interest. Inviting new owners may alter voting influence, while selling assets can release cash but reduce the scope of operations. The course links these decisions to taxation, inheritance and the possible need to compensate heirs who leave the business. Sound liquidity planning therefore considers both current operations and future succession. -- 18 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
