    "questionNumber": "POP Q27",
    "originalQuestionNumber": 27,
    "questionText": "Describe the four activities of directing a family business.",
    "questionType": "written_structured",
    "marks": 20,
    "totalMarks": 20,
    "suppliedAnswer": "Directing involves: (1) shaping the enterprise's long-term strategic direction; (2) participating in decisions with far-reaching effects; (3) deciding on significant allocation or reallocation of financial and other resources, such as large investments or selecting a chief executive; and (4) making precedent-setting decisions or choices that will be difficult to reverse. These are governance-level responsibilities. They should not be confused with routine supervision, customer service or daily inventory control. A sound family board leaves operational detail to competent managers while remaining accountable for strategic choices and oversight. Quick Revision Ownership; management; boards; accountability; competence; participative decision-making. TOPIC 9: Managing Conflict in Family Business 3.1 Managing Conflict in Family Business Conflict is a normal difference of interests or opinions. Common flashpoints in the material include hiring and exit policies, family salaries and benefits, unclear roles, ownership rights, recognition and control. These disputes are often predictable, particularly when the number of family shareholders and employees expands. Five principal guidelines are given: create an atmosphere where people can discuss problems directly; use an external neutral facilitator where necessary; arrange an agreed time and place; make communication safe for genuine listening; and avoid blaming individuals. These practices improve the chance of settling a disagreement before it becomes a permanent family division. The course also encourages discussion of unspoken concerns, uneven opportunities, cash needs and the impact of organisational change. -- 24 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
    "modelAnswer": "Directing involves: (1) shaping the enterprise's long-term strategic direction; (2) participating in decisions with far-reaching effects; (3) deciding on significant allocation or reallocation of financial and other resources, such as large investments or selecting a chief executive; and (4) making precedent-setting decisions or choices that will be difficult to reverse. These are governance-level responsibilities. They should not be confused with routine supervision, customer service or daily inventory control. A sound family board leaves operational detail to competent managers while remaining accountable for strategic choices and oversight. Quick Revision Ownership; management; boards; accountability; competence; participative decision-making. TOPIC 9: Managing Conflict in Family Business 3.1 Managing Conflict in Family Business Conflict is a normal difference of interests or opinions. Common flashpoints in the material include hiring and exit policies, family salaries and benefits, unclear roles, ownership rights, recognition and control. These disputes are often predictable, particularly when the number of family shareholders and employees expands. Five principal guidelines are given: create an atmosphere where people can discuss problems directly; use an external neutral facilitator where necessary; arrange an agreed time and place; make communication safe for genuine listening; and avoid blaming individuals. These practices improve the chance of settling a disagreement before it becomes a permanent family division. The course also encourages discussion of unspoken concerns, uneven opportunities, cash needs and the impact of organisational change. -- 24 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
