    "questionNumber": "POP Q28",
    "originalQuestionNumber": 28,
    "questionText": "State and explain five guidelines for managing conflict in a family business.",
    "questionType": "written_structured",
    "marks": 20,
    "totalMarks": 20,
    "suppliedAnswer": "The course gives five guidelines. First, develop a culture that invites appropriate direct, one-to-one discussion before tension grows. Second, use an outside neutral facilitator when parties cannot progress alone. Third, arrange a definite time and place so discussions are not left to chance. Fourth, create a safe atmosphere of genuine listening instead of defensive criticism. Fifth, avoid assigning blame and concentrate on the disagreement and how it can be settled. Employment rules, leadership roles, salaries, family benefits, liquidity and succession are common sources of tension. The aim is to address these issues fairly while protecting both the family relationship and the enterprise. Quick Revision Role definition; pay and promotion disputes; private discussion; neutral mediation; non-blaming communication. TOPIC 10: Triangulation and Its Unwanted Effects 3.1 Triangulation and Its Unwanted Effects Triangulation occurs when a family member experiencing conflict with another person repeatedly discusses the matter with a third party instead of addressing the other person directly. The listener may relieve emotional pressure temporarily but also become drawn into the dispute. The course states five unwanted effects: a third party may decide from incomplete facts, appear to interfere, seem biased, absorb too much stress, or remove the incentive for the disputants to talk to each other. The recommended approach is to use advice only to prepare for a direct conversation. Where specialist help is needed, a neutral adviser outside the family and enterprise may be preferable. Triangulation is harmful partly because the person asked to listen may receive only one side of the story. If that listener intervenes, the other party may see unfair favouritism and the original disagreement becomes a wider family quarrel. The course prefers talking directly with the person concerned after emotions settle. A third party can still assist by helping someone prepare for a respectful conversation. When impartial professional assistance is required, a counsellor or adviser outside the immediate family may avoid the conflicts of interest created by relatives taking sides. -- 25 of 33 -- PSALMDATA PSALMDATA - TELECOM AND COMPUTER CAFE Motto: I CAN DO ALL THINGS Website: http://psalmedu.com Contacts: 09163490176; 09011055579",
