    "questionText": "Define triangulation and discuss five unwanted effects on family-business relationships.",
    "questionType": "written_structured",
    "marks": 20,
    "totalMarks": 20,
    "suppliedAnswer": "Triangulation occurs when someone in disagreement with another family member repeatedly involves a third party to discuss the dispute instead of speaking directly to the person concerned. The course lists five risks. The third party may decide from only one side's account; appear to interfere where they do not belong; be regarded as biased or favouring one person; carry an excessive share of the emotional pressure; or reduce the willingness of the actual disputants to address their difference. The better course is to obtain advice, if needed, to prepare a direct discussion. A neutral outsider may help where parties cannot talk constructively. Quick Revision Triangulation; one-sided information; apparent favouritism; stress; direct dialogue. TOPIC 11: Family Governance and Corporate Governance 3.1 Family Governance and Corporate Governance Governance describes the structures and processes by which the family enterprise is directed, controlled and held accountable. The course identifies three typical components: family institutions, the board of directors, and top management. Family meetings, assemblies, councils and shareholder committees can help owners reach shared expectations. The corporate governance tasks are directing, controlling and reporting. Directing concerns strategic and long-term decisions, especially those involving substantial investment or the appointment of top management. Controlling means monitoring results against objectives. Reporting makes performance and decisions accountable to relevant parties. Proper governance can assist both strategic planning and succession planning and limit confusion between family privileges and the company's requirements. The boardroom is a major setting where the rights of owners are translated into the strategic direction of the enterprise. Family councils and meetings serve a related but different purpose: they enable family members to discuss expectations, employment, dividends and shared values before tensions reach the company's formal governing bodies. The three governance tasks - directing, controlling and reporting - work together. Long-term policy without monitoring leaves the company exposed, while constant supervision without clear strategic direction makes management reactive rather than purposeful. PAST QUESTION - 2019_1 Q4(a); 2023_1 Q4(a)",
